      "math_academy_id": "q-98175",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{0}2f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨e-1,\\,1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡1⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨e-1,\\,1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-e,\\,-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the scalar multiplication rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot ⟨1-e,\\,-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2e,\\,-2,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
